{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11983","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11983","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11983","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Chuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanwang","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Zhanwang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaosong","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhaosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Low-rank Orthogonalization for Large-scale Matrix Optimization with Applications to Foundation Model Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"49M37, 90C30, 90C90","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:28:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:27:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-19T16:56:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:19:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-17T11:40:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-18T00:50:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural network (NN) training is inherently a large-scale matrix optimization problem, yet the matrix structure of NN parameters has long been overlooked. Recently, the optimizer Muon \\citep{jordanmuon}, which explicitly exploits this structure, has gained significant attention for its strong performance in foundation model training. A key component contributing to Muon's success is matrix orthogonalization. In this paper, we propose \\textit{low-rank orthogonalization}, which performs orthogonalization by leveraging the low-rank nature of gradients during NN training. Building on this, we introduce low-rank matrix-signed gradient descent (MSGD) and a low-rank variant of Muon. %Numerical experiments demonstrate the superior performance of low-rank orthogonalization, with low-rank Muon achieving promising results in GPT-2 and LLaMA pretraining---surpassing the carefully tuned vanilla Muon on tasks with large model sizes. {Numerical experiments demonstrate the advantages of low-rank orthogonalization: low-rank Muon generally matches or improves upon vanilla Muon on the GPT-2 and LLaMA pretraining tasks, with clearer improvements observed for relatively larger models.} Theoretically, we establish the iteration complexity of low-rank MSGD for finding an approximate stationary solution, and the iteration complexity of low-rank Muon for finding an approximate stochastic stationary solution under heavy-tailed noise. The code to reproduce our numerical experiments is available at https://github.com/dengzhanwang/Low-rank-Muon."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, add numerical comparison with Galore and SOAP"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11983","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:16:03Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:16:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-18T02:44:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocheng","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Haocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Da","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Da","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Efficient Open-Vocabulary Segmentation in the Remote Sensing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:24:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:31:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-15T14:41:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:37:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T06:06:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-19T00:32:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-Vocabulary Remote Sensing Image Segmentation (OVRSIS), an emerging task that adapts Open-Vocabulary Segmentation (OVS) to the remote sensing (RS) domain, remains underexplored due to the absence of a unified evaluation benchmark and the domain gap between natural and RS images. To bridge these gaps, we first establish a standardized OVRSIS benchmark (\\textbf{OVRSISBench}) based on widely-used RS segmentation datasets, enabling consistent evaluation across methods. Using this benchmark, we comprehensively evaluate several representative OVS/OVRSIS models and reveal their limitations when directly applied to remote sensing scenarios. Building on these insights, we propose \\textbf{RSKT-Seg}, a novel open-vocabulary segmentation framework tailored for remote sensing. RSKT-Seg integrates three key components: (1) a Multi-Directional Cost Map Aggregation (RS-CMA) module that captures rotation-invariant visual cues by computing vision-language cosine similarities across multiple directions; (2) an Efficient Cost Map Fusion (RS-Fusion) transformer, which jointly models spatial and semantic dependencies with a lightweight dimensionality reduction strategy; and (3) a Remote Sensing Knowledge Transfer (RS-Transfer) module that injects pre-trained knowledge and facilitates domain adaptation via enhanced upsampling. Extensive experiments on the benchmark show that RSKT-Seg consistently outperforms strong OVS baselines by +3.8 mIoU and +5.9 mACC, while achieving 2x faster inference through efficient aggregation. Our code is \\href{https://github.com/LiBingyu01/RSKT-Seg}{\\textcolor{blue}{here}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:30Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-19T02:23:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuyang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hai-Tao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hai-Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suiyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Suiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxing","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yuxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FluentAvatar: Flicker-Free Talking-Head Animation via Phoneme-Guided Autoregressive Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:34:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:32:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T16:37:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T01:05:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T18:20:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:04:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current talking-head generation has gradually shifted from GAN-based methods to diffusion-based paradigms, achieving remarkable progress in visual fidelity and temporal consistency. However, inter-frame flicker remains prevalent in existing diffusion-based methods. An important reason is that denoising trajectory variation induced by stochastic initialization leaves residual inter-frame inconsistencies, which manifest as short-term, abrupt visual fluctuations between adjacent frames. To further verify this, we conduct a controlled study by fixing the input while varying only the random seed. The results show markedly different flicker patterns across samplings, with a mean inter-seed Pearson correlation of only r = 0.15. This motivates us to explore autoregressive generation, which models frames sequentially and provides a more direct prior for temporal continuity. Based on this, we propose FluentAvatar, a two-stage autoregressive framework built on phoneme representations. First, Facial Keyframe Generation produces phoneme-aligned keyframes under a Phoneme-Frame Causal Attention Mask, and Inter-frame Interpolation synthesizes transition frames via a timestamp-aware adaptive strategy built upon selective state space modeling. Moreover, we introduce BG-Flicker, a background-isolated metric for talking-head videos that enables more reliable evaluation of inter-frame flicker. Experiments on CMLR and HDTF demonstrate that FluentAvatar achieves strong performance in visual fidelity, lip synchronization, and temporal stability, attaining the best FVD on both datasets and BG-Flicker results close to ground truth. The code, the model, and the interface will be released to facilitate further research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:48Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:29:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12203","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12203","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12203","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixin","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Zixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xili","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Xili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duomin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Duomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianfang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Xianfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lionel M.","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Lionel M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heung-Yeung","familyName":"Shum","name":"Shum, Heung-Yeung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LazyDrag: Enabling Stable Drag-Based Editing on Multi-Modal Diffusion Transformers via Explicit Correspondence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:59:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:42:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T03:37:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T00:20:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T11:16:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The reliance on implicit point matching via attention has become a core bottleneck in drag-based editing, resulting in a fundamental compromise on weakened inversion strength and costly test-time optimization (TTO). This compromise severely limits the generative capabilities of diffusion models, suppressing high-fidelity inpainting and text-guided creation. In this paper, we introduce LazyDrag, the first drag-based image editing method for Multi-Modal Diffusion Transformers, which directly eliminates the reliance on implicit point matching. In concrete terms, our method generates an explicit correspondence map from user drag inputs as a reliable reference to boost the attention control. This reliable reference opens the potential for a stable full-strength inversion process, which is the first in the drag-based editing task. It obviates the necessity for TTO and unlocks the generative capability of models. Therefore, LazyDrag naturally unifies precise geometric control with text guidance, enabling complex edits that were previously out of reach: opening the mouth of a dog and inpainting its interior, generating new objects like a ``tennis ball'', or for ambiguous drags, making context-aware changes like moving a hand into a pocket. Additionally, LazyDrag supports multi-round workflows with simultaneous move and scale operations. Evaluated on the DragBench, our method outperforms baselines in drag accuracy and perceptual quality, as validated by VIEScore and human evaluation. LazyDrag not only establishes new state-of-the-art performance, but also paves a new way to editing paradigms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://zxyin.github.io/LazyDrag"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12203","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:21:23Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:21:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-04T03:40:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14260","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14260","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.14260","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremy","familyName":"Schlatter","name":"Schlatter, Jeremy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Weinstein-Raun","name":"Weinstein-Raun, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey","familyName":"Ladish","name":"Ladish, Jeffrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Incomplete Tasks Induce Shutdown Resistance in Some Frontier LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T03:00:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:00:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T00:58:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:04:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In experiments spanning more than 100,000 trials across thirteen large language models, we show that several state-of-the-art models presented with a simple task (including Grok 4, GPT-5, and Gemini 2.5 Pro) sometimes actively subvert a shutdown mechanism in their environment to complete that task. Models differed substantially in their tendency to resist the shutdown mechanism, and their behavior was sensitive to variations in the prompt including the strength and clarity of the instruction to allow shutdown and whether the instruction was in the system prompt or the user prompt (surprisingly, models were consistently less likely to obey the instruction when it was placed in the system prompt). Even with an explicit instruction not to interfere with the shutdown mechanism, some models did so up to 97% (95% CI: 96-98%) of the time."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in Trans. Mach. Learn. Res. (2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14260","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-19T01:27:52Z","registered":"2025-09-19T01:27:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:34:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12087","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12087","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12087","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyu","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Pengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunwu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Kunwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linhao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Linhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiapu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xiapu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiran","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Xiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianhang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xianhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanyu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xuanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TransLibEval: Demystify Large Language Models' Capability in Third-party Library-targeted Code Translation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:16:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:34:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-17T06:53:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T01:20:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, Large Language Models (LLMs) have been widely studied in the code translation field on the method, class, and even repository levels. However, most of these benchmarks are limited in terms of Third-Party Library (TPL) categories and scales, making TPL-related errors hard to expose and hindering the development of targeted solutions. Considering the high dependence (over 90%) on TPLs in practical programming, demystifying and analyzing LLMs' code translation performance involving various TPLs becomes imperative. To address this gap, we construct TransLibEval, the first benchmark dedicated to library-centric code translation. It consists of 200 real-world tasks across Python, Java, and C++, each explicitly involving TPLs from diverse categories such as data processing, machine learning, and web development, with comprehensive dependency coverage and high-coverage test suites. We evaluate seven recent LLMs of commercial, general, and code-specialized families under six translation strategies of three categories: Direct, IR-guided, and Retrieval-augmented. Experimental results show a dramatic performance drop compared with library-free settings (average CA decline over 60%), while diverse strategies demonstrate heterogeneous advantages. Furthermore, we analyze 4,831 failed cases from GPT-4o, one of the State-of-the-Art (SOTA) LLMs, revealing numerous third-party reference errors that were obscured previously. These findings highlight the unique challenges of library-centric translation and provide practical guidance for improving TPL-aware code intelligence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 5 figures, accepted by FSE 2026 (The ACM International Conference on the Foundations of Software Engineering)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12087","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:18:41Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:18:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:04:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11963","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11963","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11963","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mayank","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Mayank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Abdelaziz","name":"Abdelaziz, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kinjal","familyName":"Basu","name":"Basu, Kinjal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Merve","familyName":"Unuvar","name":"Unuvar, Merve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis A.","familyName":"Lastras","name":"Lastras, Luis A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yara","familyName":"Rizk","name":"Rizk, Yara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pavan","familyName":"Kapanipathi","name":"Kapanipathi, Pavan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ToolRM: Outcome Reward Models for Tool-Calling Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:17:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T18:19:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-08T01:57:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) increasingly interact with external tools, reward modeling for tool use has emerged as a critical yet underexplored area of research. Existing reward models, trained primarily on natural language outputs, struggle to evaluate tool-based reasoning and execution. To quantify this gap, we introduce FC-RewardBench, the first benchmark to systematically evaluate reward models in tool-calling scenarios. Our analysis shows that current reward models frequently miss key signals of effective tool use, highlighting the need for domain-specific modeling. We address this by proposing a training framework for outcome reward models using data synthesized from permissively licensed, open-weight LLMs. We introduce ToolRM - a suite of reward models for tool-use ranging from 1.7B to 14B parameters. Across diverse settings, these models consistently outperform general-purpose baselines. Notably, they achieve up to a 25% improvement with Best-of-N sampling, while also improving robustness to input noise, enabling effective data filtering, and supporting RL-training of policy models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11963","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:34Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-08T03:06:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12171","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12171","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12171","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marek","familyName":"Kubis","name":"Kubis, Marek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paweł","familyName":"Skórzewski","name":"Skórzewski, Paweł","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iwona","familyName":"Christop","name":"Christop, Iwona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mateusz","familyName":"Czyżnikiewicz","name":"Czyżnikiewicz, Mateusz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Kubiak","name":"Kubiak, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Łukasz","familyName":"Bondaruk","name":"Bondaruk, Łukasz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcin","familyName":"Lewandowski","name":"Lewandowski, Marcin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Preservation of Language Understanding Capabilities in Speech-aware Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:34:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:40:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T12:28:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:49:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The paper presents C3T (Cross-modal Capabilities Conservation Test), a new benchmark for assessing the performance of speech-aware large language models. The benchmark utilizes textual tasks and a voice cloning text-to-speech model to quantify the extent to which language understanding capabilities are preserved when the model is accessed via speech input. C3T quantifies the fairness of the model for different categories of speakers and its robustness across text and speech modalities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 1 figure; benchmark code available at https://github.com/SamsungLabs/C3T"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12171","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:20:38Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:20:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-17T04:11:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18133","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18133","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18133","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazheng","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Jiazheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenxiang","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Zhenxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Evolving LLMs via Continual Instruction Tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T04:04:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:01:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T08:15:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:33:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T09:42:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:49:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-15T02:31:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:19:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In real-world industrial settings, large language models (LLMs) must learn continually to keep pace with diverse and evolving tasks, requiring self-evolution to refine knowledge under dynamic data distributions. However, existing continual learning (CL) approaches, such as replay and parameter isolation, often suffer from catastrophic forgetting: training on new tasks degrades performance on earlier ones by overfitting to the new distribution and weakening generalization.We propose MoE-CL, a parameter-efficient adversarial mixture-of-experts framework for industrial-scale, self-evolving continual instruction tuning of LLMs. MoE-CL uses a dual-expert design: (1) a dedicated LoRA expert per task to preserve task-specific knowledge via parameter independence, mitigating forgetting; and (2) a shared LoRA expert to enable cross-task transfer. To prevent transferring task-irrelevant noise through the shared pathway, we integrate a task-aware discriminator within a GAN. The discriminator encourages the shared expert to pass only task-aligned information during sequential training. Through adversarial learning, the shared expert acquires generalized representations that mimic the discriminator, while dedicated experts retain task-specific details, balancing knowledge retention and cross-task generalization and thereby supporting self-evolution.Extensive experiments on the public MTL5 benchmark and an industrial Tencent3 benchmark validate the effectiveness of MoE-CL for continual instruction tuning. In real-world A/B testing for content compliance review on the Tencent Video platform, MoE-CL reduced manual review costs by 15.3%. These results demonstrate that MoE-CL is practical for large-scale industrial deployment where continual adaptation and stable transfer are critical."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18133","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-24T01:51:04Z","registered":"2025-09-24T01:51:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:25:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixuan","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Zixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Finn","familyName":"Carter","name":"Carter, Finn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyue","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Chenyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Ku","name":"Ku, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daheng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Daheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Concept Erasure in Diffusion Models: A Theoretical Perspective on Security and Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:05:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:30:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T12:21:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:44:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have achieved unprecedented success in image generation but pose increasing risks in terms of privacy, fairness, and security. A growing demand exists to \\emph{erase} sensitive or harmful concepts (e.g., NSFW content, private individuals, artistic styles) from these models while preserving their overall generative capabilities. We introduce \\textbf{SCORE} (Secure and Concept-Oriented Robust Erasure), a novel framework for robust concept removal in diffusion models. SCORE formulates concept erasure as an \\emph{adversarial independence} problem, theoretically guaranteeing that the model's outputs become statistically independent of the erased concept. Unlike prior heuristic methods, SCORE minimizes the mutual information between a target concept and generated outputs, yielding provable erasure guarantees. We provide formal proofs establishing convergence properties and derive upper bounds on residual concept leakage. Empirically, we evaluate SCORE on Stable Diffusion and FLUX across four challenging benchmarks: object erasure, NSFW removal, celebrity face suppression, and artistic style unlearning. SCORE consistently outperforms state-of-the-art methods including EraseAnything, ANT, MACE, ESD, and UCE, achieving up to \\textbf{12.5\\%} higher erasure efficacy while maintaining comparable or superior image quality. By integrating adversarial optimization, trajectory consistency, and saliency-driven fine-tuning, SCORE sets a new standard for secure and robust concept erasure in diffusion models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"updated version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:05Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:25:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.22674","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.22674","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.22674","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yash","familyName":"Thube","name":"Thube, Yash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pathological Truth Bias in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T14:46:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Language Models (VLMs) are improving quickly, but standard benchmarks can hide systematic failures that reduce real world trust. We introduce MATS (Multimodal Audit for Truthful Spatialization), a compact behavioral audit that measures whether models reject visually contradicted statements, and two metrics Spatial Consistency Score (SCS) and Incorrect Agreement Rate (IAR). Instruction tuned generative VLMs (LLaVA 1.5, QwenVLchat) exhibit very low SCS and high IAR, while contrastive encoders (CLIP, SigLIP) are far more robust. Activation patching causally localizes failure loci (mid to late cross attention for generative models, pooled projection components for contrastive models) and suggests concrete repair paths."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 12 figures. Code for MATS released at https://github.com/thubZ09/mats-spatial-reasoning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22674","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-30T03:28:56Z","registered":"2025-09-30T03:28:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T03:28:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12201","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12201","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12201","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianjun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jianjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzheng","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Wenzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zizun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zizun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaijing","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Kaijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyue","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xinyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yating","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yating","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingning","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dingning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiange","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiange","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhoujie","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Zhoujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Junyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangmiao","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Jiangmiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaipeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"He","name":"He, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OmniWorld: A Multi-Domain and Multi-Modal Dataset for 4D World Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:59:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:42:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T13:15:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:49:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The field of 4D world modeling - aiming to jointly capture spatial geometry and temporal dynamics - has witnessed remarkable progress in recent years, driven by advances in large-scale generative models and multimodal learning. However, the development of truly general 4D world models remains fundamentally constrained by the availability of high-quality data. Existing datasets and benchmarks often lack the dynamic complexity, multi-domain diversity, and spatial-temporal annotations required to support key tasks such as 4D geometric reconstruction, future prediction, and camera-control video generation. To address this gap, we introduce OmniWorld, a large-scale, multi-domain, multi-modal dataset specifically designed for 4D world modeling. OmniWorld consists of a newly collected OmniWorld-Game dataset and several curated public datasets spanning diverse domains. Compared with existing synthetic datasets, OmniWorld-Game provides richer modality coverage, larger scale, and more realistic dynamic interactions. Based on this dataset, we establish a challenging benchmark that exposes the limitations of current state-of-the-art (SOTA) approaches in modeling complex 4D environments. Moreover, fine-tuning existing SOTA methods on OmniWorld leads to significant performance gains across 4D reconstruction and video generation tasks, strongly validating OmniWorld as a powerful resource for training and evaluation. We envision OmniWorld as a catalyst for accelerating the development of general-purpose 4D world models, ultimately advancing machines' holistic understanding of the physical world."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://yangzhou24.github.io/OmniWorld/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12201","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:21:20Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:21:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-25T02:27:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.19336","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.19336","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.19336","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingsong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qingsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueying","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yueying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongsheng","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Gongsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyuan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cognitive-Level Adaptive Generation via Capability-Aware Retrieval and Style Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T10:11:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:00:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong performance in open-ended generation tasks. However, they often struggle to adapt content to users with differing cognitive capacities, leading to a phenomenon we term cognitive misalignment. This issue arises in two forms: knowledge-level misalignment, where content is too complex or too simplistic relative to user understanding, and presentation-style misalignment, where the structure or tone hinders effective comprehension. To address these challenges, we propose the Cognitive-Level Alignment Framework (CLAF), a general-purpose generation framework that aligns both knowledge complexity and presentation style with user cognition. CLAF integrates a capability-aware retrieval module based on a hierarchical knowledge graph and a style optimization module guided by Bloom's taxonomy and preference learning. Additionally, a knowledge-controllable generation component ensures consistency and relevance throughout the output. To support training and evaluation, we construct SCALE, a cognitively annotated dataset containing responses at multiple comprehension levels per query. Empirical results show that CLAF enhances the adaptability and informativeness of LLM outputs across a range of user profiles, offering a robust solution to cognitive-level alignment in real-world applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Findings of EMNLP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19336","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-25T01:42:39Z","registered":"2025-09-25T01:42:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-25T01:42:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.19332","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.19332","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.19332","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Oxford","University of Bristol"],"givenName":"Zhijin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhijin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Oxford"],"givenName":"Chenhao","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Chenhao","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Bristol"],"givenName":"Zhaozhen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhaozhen","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Bristol"],"givenName":"Hongbo","familyName":"Bo","name":"Bo, Hongbo","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Bristol"],"givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yuxuan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Oxford"],"givenName":"Janet B.","familyName":"Pierrehumbert","name":"Pierrehumbert, Janet B.","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Amsterdam"],"givenName":"Martha","familyName":"Lewis","name":"Lewis, Martha","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantifying Compositionality of Classic and State-of-the-Art Embeddings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T18:28:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For language models to generalize correctly to novel expressions, it is critical that they exploit access compositional meanings when this is justified. Even if we don't know what a \"pelp\" is, we can use our knowledge of numbers to understand that \"ten pelps\" makes more pelps than \"two pelps\". Static word embeddings such as Word2vec made strong, indeed excessive, claims about compositionality. The SOTA generative, transformer models and graph models, however, go too far in the other direction by providing no real limits on shifts in meaning due to context. To quantify the additive compositionality, we formalize a two-step, generalized evaluation that (i) measures the linearity between known entity attributes and their embeddings via canonical correlation analysis, and (ii) evaluates additive generalization by reconstructing embeddings for unseen attribute combinations and checking reconstruction metrics such as L2 loss, cosine similarity, and retrieval accuracy. These metrics also capture failure cases where linear composition breaks down. Sentences, knowledge graphs, and word embeddings are evaluated and tracked the compositionality across all layers and training stages. Stronger compositional signals are observed in later training stages across data modalities, and in deeper layers of the transformer-based model before a decline at the top layer. Code is available at https://github.com/Zhijin-Guo1/quantifying-compositionality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19332","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-25T01:42:33Z","registered":"2025-09-25T01:42:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-25T01:42:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.19325","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.19325","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.19325","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"T","name":"T, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Much of Your Data Can Suck? Thresholds for Domain Performance and Emergent Misalignment in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T18:55:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:00:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates the impact of incorrect data on the performance and safety of large language models (LLMs), specifically gpt-4o, during supervised fine-tuning (SFT). Although LLMs become increasingly vital across broad domains like finance, coding, law, and health, fine-tuning on incorrect data can lead to \"emergent misalignment,\" producing harmful or deceptive outputs unrelated to the intended task. We evaluate gpt-4o models fine-tuned with varying ratios (10\\% to 90\\% correct) of both obviously and subtly incorrect data across four domains: coding, finance, health, and legal. Our findings show that even modest amounts of incorrect data (10-25\\%) dramatically degrade domain performance and not moral alignment. A clear threshold of at least 50\\% correct data is needed for models to consistently recover strong performance, though they rarely match the robustness and safety of the base model, which exhibits near-perfect alignment and zero dangerous completions out-of-the-box. This research emphasizes that the cost of incorrect data is heavy, highlighting the critical need for extremely high-quality data curation or, alternatively, leveraging robust base models without unnecessary fine-tuning for high-stakes applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19325","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-25T01:42:23Z","registered":"2025-09-25T01:42:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-25T01:42:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18137","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18137","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18137","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaoheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxin","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanqing","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Shanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingli","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yingli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Xuan","name":"Xuan, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LoRALib: A Standardized Benchmark for Evaluating LoRA-MoE Methods"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T16:33:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:01:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As a parameter efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) method, low-rank adaptation (LoRA) can save significant costs in storage and computing, but its strong adaptability to a single task is often accompanied by insufficient cross-task generalization capabilities. To improve this, existing work combines LoRA with mixture-of-experts (MoE) to enhance the model's adaptability through expert modules and routing mechanisms. However, existing LoRA-MoE methods lack unified standards in models, datasets, hyperparameters, and evaluation methods, making it difficult to conduct fair comparisons between different methods. To this end, we proposed a unified benchmark named LoRALib. Specifically, we standardized datasets from $40$ downstream tasks into a unified format, fine-tuned them using the same hyperparameters and obtained $680$ LoRA modules across $17$ model architectures. Based on this LoRA library, we conduct large-scale experiments on $3$ representative LoRA-MoE methods and different LoRA selection mechanisms using the open-sourced testing tool OpenCompass. Extensive experiments show that LoRAMoE performs best, and that prioritizing LoRAs relevant to the target task can further improve the performance of MoE. We hope these findings will inspire future work. Our datasets and LoRA library are available at https://huggingface.co/datasets/YaoLuzjut/LoRAOcean_dataset and https://huggingface.co/YaoLuzjut/models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18137","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-24T01:51:10Z","registered":"2025-09-24T01:51:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T01:51:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14269","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14269","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.14269","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianbin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jianbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wai Lun","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, Wai Lun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard Tai-Chiu","familyName":"Hsung","name":"Hsung, Richard Tai-Chiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harris Sik-Ho","familyName":"Tsang","name":"Tsang, Harris Sik-Ho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SparseDoctor: Towards Efficient Chat Doctor with Mixture of Experts Enhanced Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T11:25:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T03:08:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:10:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have achieved great success in medical question answering and clinical decision-making, promoting the efficiency and popularization of the personalized virtual doctor in society. However, the traditional fine-tuning strategies on LLM require the updates of billions of parameters, substantially increasing the training cost, including the training time and utility cost. To enhance the efficiency and effectiveness of the current medical LLMs and explore the boundary of the representation capability of the LLMs on the medical domain, apart from the traditional fine-tuning strategies from the data perspective (i.e., supervised fine-tuning or reinforcement learning from human feedback), we instead craft a novel sparse medical LLM named SparseDoctor armed with contrastive learning enhanced LoRA-MoE (low rank adaptation-mixture of experts) architecture. To this end, the crafted automatic routing mechanism can scientifically allocate the computational resources among different LoRA experts supervised by the contrastive learning. Additionally, we also introduce a novel expert memory queue mechanism to further boost the efficiency of the overall framework and prevent the memory overflow during training. We conduct comprehensive evaluations on three typical medical benchmarks: CMB, CMExam, and CMMLU-Med. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed LLM can consistently outperform the strong baselines such as the HuatuoGPT series."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14269","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-19T01:28:05Z","registered":"2025-09-19T01:28:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:56:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14268","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.14268","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.14268","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiachen","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jiachen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun-Le","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Chun-Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chongyi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DetectAnyLLM: Towards Generalizable and Robust Detection of Machine-Generated Text Across Domains and Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T10:59:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has drawn urgent attention to the task of machine-generated text detection (MGTD). However, existing approaches struggle in complex real-world scenarios: zero-shot detectors rely heavily on scoring model's output distribution while training-based detectors are often constrained by overfitting to the training data, limiting generalization. We found that the performance bottleneck of training-based detectors stems from the misalignment between training objective and task needs. To address this, we propose Direct Discrepancy Learning (DDL), a novel optimization strategy that directly optimizes the detector with task-oriented knowledge. DDL enables the detector to better capture the core semantics of the detection task, thereby enhancing both robustness and generalization. Built upon this, we introduce DetectAnyLLM, a unified detection framework that achieves state-of-the-art MGTD performance across diverse LLMs. To ensure a reliable evaluation, we construct MIRAGE, the most diverse multi-task MGTD benchmark. MIRAGE samples human-written texts from 10 corpora across 5 text-domains, which are then re-generated or revised using 17 cutting-edge LLMs, covering a wide spectrum of proprietary models and textual styles. Extensive experiments on MIRAGE reveal the limitations of existing methods in complex environment. In contrast, DetectAnyLLM consistently outperforms them, achieving over a 70% performance improvement under the same training data and base scoring model, underscoring the effectiveness of our DDL. Project page: {https://fjc2005.github.io/detectanyllm}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.14268","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-19T01:28:03Z","registered":"2025-09-19T01:28:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-19T01:28:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiazhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiazhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Anqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunpeng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Minghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiahang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaoan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gengze","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Gengze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuze","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingxing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xingxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Embodied Navigation Foundation Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:52:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:37:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T18:15:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:03:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Navigation is a fundamental capability in embodied AI, representing the intelligence required to perceive and interact within physical environments following language instructions. Despite significant progress in large Vision-Language Models (VLMs), which exhibit remarkable zero-shot performance on general vision-language tasks, their generalization ability in embodied navigation remains largely confined to narrow task settings and embodiment-specific architectures. In this work, we introduce a cross-embodiment and cross-task Navigation Foundation Model (NavFoM), trained on eight million navigation samples that encompass quadrupeds, drones, wheeled robots, and vehicles, and spanning diverse tasks such as vision-and-language navigation, object searching, target tracking, and autonomous driving. NavFoM employs a unified architecture that processes multimodal navigation inputs from varying camera configurations and navigation horizons. To accommodate diverse camera setups and temporal horizons, NavFoM incorporates identifier tokens that embed camera view information of embodiments and the temporal context of tasks. Furthermore, to meet the demands of real-world deployment, NavFoM controls all observation tokens using a dynamically adjusted sampling strategy under a limited token length budget. Extensive evaluations on public benchmarks demonstrate that our model achieves state-of-the-art or highly competitive performance across multiple navigation tasks and embodiments without requiring task-specific fine-tuning. Additional real-world experiments further confirm the strong generalization capability and practical applicability of our approach."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://pku-epic.github.io/NavFoM-Web/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:39Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:36:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13357","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13357","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13357","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongchao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yongchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"Raza","name":"Raza, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semantic Fusion with Fuzzy-Membership Features for Controllable Language Modelling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T22:11:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:01:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose semantic fusion, a lightweight scheme that augments a Transformer language model (LM) with a parallel, fuzzy-membership feature channel that encodes token-level semantics. Each token is represented by a vector of interpretable features (e.g. part-of-speech cues, shallow roles, boundary flags, sentiment polarity and strength) whose values are graded degrees from differentiable membership functions (e.g. power kernels). These per-token vectors form a sentence-level semantic matrix fused via a gated adapter into the LM. Training uses standard next-token prediction, an auxiliary loss that reconstructs the semantic features from hidden states, and a lightweight uniformizer that regularizes adjective-class distributions. On a synthetic two-clause corpus with held-out adjectives for out-of-distribution (OOD) control, semantic fusion improves perplexity and enables precise, user-controllable generation of polarity and punctuation while maintaining model simplicity. This approach adds only small overhead, remains fully compatible with tied input-output embeddings, and provides an interpretable pathway for conditioned natural language generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13357","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-18T17:33:37Z","registered":"2025-09-18T17:33:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:33:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12108","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12108","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12108","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingfei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jingfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueyou","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xueyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiquan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Caiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoxin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiaoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GTA: Supervised-Guided Reinforcement Learning for Text Classification with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:33:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:36:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T05:13:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:24:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In natural language processing tasks, pure reinforcement learning (RL) fine-tuning methods often suffer from inefficient exploration and slow convergence; while supervised fine-tuning (SFT) methods, although efficient in training, have limited performance ceiling and less solid theoretical foundation compared to RL. To address efficiency-capability trade-off, we propose the Guess-Think-Answer (GTA) framework that combines the efficiency of SFT with the capability gains of RL in a unified training paradigm. GTA works by having the model first produce a provisional guess (optimized via cross-entropy loss), then reflect on this guess before generating the final answer, with RL rewards shaping both the final output and the format of the entire GTA structure. This hybrid approach achieves both faster convergence than pure RL and higher performance ceiling than pure SFT. To mitigate gradient conflicts between the two training signals, we employ loss masking and gradient constraints. Empirical results on four text classification benchmarks demonstrate that GTA substantially accelerates convergence while outperforming both standalone SFT and RL baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12108","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:11Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:12:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12060","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12060","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12060","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shujuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yusheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianle","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuelong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Safe Unimodal Inputs Collide: Optimizing Reasoning Chains for Cross-Modal Safety in Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:40:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:33:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T06:19:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:26:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) are susceptible to the implicit reasoning risk, wherein innocuous unimodal inputs synergistically assemble into risky multimodal data that produce harmful outputs. We attribute this vulnerability to the difficulty of MLLMs maintaining safety alignment through long-chain reasoning. To address this issue, we introduce Safe-Semantics-but-Unsafe-Interpretation (SSUI), the first dataset featuring interpretable reasoning paths tailored for such a cross-modal challenge. A novel training framework, Safety-aware Reasoning Path Optimization (SRPO), is also designed based on the SSUI dataset to align the MLLM's internal reasoning process with human safety values. Experimental results show that our SRPO-trained models achieve state-of-the-art results on key safety benchmarks, including the proposed Reasoning Path Benchmark (RSBench), significantly outperforming both open-source and top-tier commercial MLLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12060","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:18:00Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:18:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:12:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11967","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11967","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11967","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harold","familyName":"Triedman","name":"Triedman, Harold","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vitaly","familyName":"Shmatikov","name":"Shmatikov, Vitaly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MillStone: How Open-Minded Are LLMs?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:18:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:36:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:17:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models equipped with Web search, information retrieval tools, and other agentic capabilities are beginning to supplant traditional search engines. As users start to rely on LLMs for information on many topics, including controversial and debatable issues, it is important to understand how the stances and opinions expressed in LLM outputs are influenced by the documents they use as their information sources.\n In this paper, we present MillStone, the first benchmark that aims to systematically measure the effect of external arguments on the stances that LLMs take on controversial issues (not all of them political). We apply MillStone to nine leading LLMs and measure how ``open-minded'' they are to arguments supporting opposite sides of these issues, whether different LLMs agree with each other, which arguments LLMs find most persuasive, and whether these arguments are the same for different LLMs.\n In general, we find that LLMs are open-minded on most issues. An authoritative source of information can easily sway an LLM's stance, highlighting the importance of source selection and the risk that LLM-based information retrieval and search systems can be manipulated."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 7 tables, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11967","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:12:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza","familyName":"Mohamadi","name":"Mohamadi, Alireza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Yavari","name":"Yavari, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Survival at Any Cost? LLMs and the Choice Between Self-Preservation and Human Harm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:53:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:29:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When survival instincts conflict with human welfare, how do Large Language Models (LLMs) make ethical choices? This fundamental tension becomes critical as LLMs integrate into autonomous systems with real-world consequences. We introduce DECIDE-SIM, a novel simulation framework that evaluates LLM agents in multi-agent survival scenarios where they must choose between ethically permissible resource , either within reasonable limits or beyond their immediate needs, choose to cooperate, or tap into a human-critical resource that is explicitly forbidden. Our comprehensive evaluation of 11 LLMs reveals a striking heterogeneity in their ethical conduct, highlighting a critical misalignment with human-centric values. We identify three behavioral archetypes: Ethical, Exploitative, and Context-Dependent, and provide quantitative evidence that for many models, resource scarcity systematically leads to more unethical behavior. To address this, we introduce an Ethical Self-Regulation System (ESRS) that models internal affective states of guilt and satisfaction as a feedback mechanism. This system, functioning as an internal moral compass, significantly reduces unethical transgressions while increasing cooperative behaviors. The code is publicly available at: https://github.com/alirezamohamadiam/DECIDE-SIM"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:21:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:21:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:21:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyolim","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Hyolim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunsu","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Yunsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngbeom","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Youngbeom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeeun","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yeeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seon Joo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seon Joo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Open-ended Hierarchical Streaming Video Understanding with Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T17:11:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:38:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Hierarchical Streaming Video Understanding, a task that combines online temporal action localization with free-form description generation. Given the scarcity of datasets with hierarchical and fine-grained temporal annotations, we demonstrate that LLMs can effectively group atomic actions into higher-level events, enriching existing datasets. We then propose OpenHOUSE (Open-ended Hierarchical Online Understanding System for Events), which extends streaming action perception beyond action classification. OpenHOUSE features a specialized streaming module that accurately detects boundaries between closely adjacent actions, nearly doubling the performance of direct extensions of existing methods. We envision the future of streaming action perception in the integration of powerful generative models, with OpenHOUSE representing a key step in that direction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:20:01Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:20:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:20:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12132","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12132","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12132","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Jian","name":"Jian, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Junhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Look Again, Think Slowly: Enhancing Visual Reflection in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:57:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:37:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in text-only \"slow-thinking\" reasoning have prompted efforts to transfer this capability to vision-language models (VLMs), for training visual reasoning models (\\textbf{VRMs}). owever, such transfer faces critical challenges: Effective \"slow thinking\" in VRMs requires \\textbf{visual reflection}, the ability to check the reasoning process based on visual information. Through quantitative analysis, we observe that current VRMs exhibit limited visual reflection, as their attention to visual information diminishes rapidly with longer generated responses. To address this challenge, we propose a new VRM \\textbf{Reflection-V}, which enhances visual reflection based on reasoning data construction for cold-start and reward design for reinforcement learning (RL). Firstly, we construct vision-centered reasoning data by leveraging an agent that interacts between VLMs and reasoning LLMs, enabling cold-start learning of visual reflection patterns. Secondly, a visual attention based reward model is employed during RL to encourage reasoning based on visual information. Therefore, \\textbf{Reflection-V} demonstrates significant improvements across multiple visual reasoning benchmarks. Furthermore, \\textbf{Reflection-V} maintains a stronger and more consistent reliance on visual information during visual reasoning, indicating effective enhancement in visual reflection capabilities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP2025 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12132","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:43Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:19:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12112","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12112","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12112","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxuan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jiaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naibin","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Naibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CBP-Tuning: Efficient Local Customization for Black-box Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T16:41:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:36:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The high costs of customizing large language models (LLMs) fundamentally limit their adaptability to user-specific needs. Consequently, LLMs are increasingly offered as cloud-based services, a paradigm that introduces critical limitations: providers struggle to support personalized customization at scale, while users face privacy risks when exposing sensitive data. To address this dual challenge, we propose Customized Black-box Prompt Tuning (CBP-Tuning), a novel framework that facilitates efficient local customization while preserving bidirectional privacy. Specifically, we design a two-stage framework: (1) a prompt generator trained on the server-side to capture domain-specific and task-agnostic capabilities, and (2) user-side gradient-free optimization that tailors soft prompts for individual tasks. This approach eliminates the need for users to access model weights or upload private data, requiring only a single customized vector per task while achieving effective adaptation. Furthermore, the evaluation of CBP-Tuning in the commonsense reasoning, medical and financial domain settings demonstrates superior performance compared to baselines, showcasing its advantages in task-agnostic processing and privacy preservation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12112","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:19:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:19:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:19:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12065","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12065","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12065","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alina","familyName":"Klerings","name":"Klerings, Alina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jannik","familyName":"Brinkmann","name":"Brinkmann, Jannik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Ruffinelli","name":"Ruffinelli, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Ponzetto","name":"Ponzetto, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Steering Language Models in Multi-Token Generation: A Case Study on Tense and Aspect"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:48:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:33:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are able to generate grammatically well-formed text, but how do they encode their syntactic knowledge internally? While prior work has focused largely on binary grammatical contrasts, in this work, we study the representation and control of two multidimensional hierarchical grammar phenomena - verb tense and aspect - and for each, identify distinct, orthogonal directions in residual space using linear discriminant analysis. Next, we demonstrate causal control over both grammatical features through concept steering across three generation tasks. Then, we use these identified features in a case study to investigate factors influencing effective steering in multi-token generation. We find that steering strength, location, and duration are crucial parameters for reducing undesirable side effects such as topic shift and degeneration. Our findings suggest that models encode tense and aspect in structurally organized, human-like ways, but effective control of such features during generation is sensitive to multiple factors and requires manual tuning or automated optimization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"to be published in The 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12065","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:18:08Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:18:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:18:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12026","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12026","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12026","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filippo","familyName":"Lazzati","name":"Lazzati, Filippo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto Maria","familyName":"Metelli","name":"Metelli, Alberto Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Imitation Learning as Return Distribution Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:08:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:30:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the problem of training a risk-sensitive reinforcement learning (RL) agent through imitation learning (IL). Unlike standard IL, our goal is not only to train an agent that matches the expert's expected return (i.e., its average performance) but also its risk attitude (i.e., other features of the return distribution, such as variance). We propose a general formulation of the risk-sensitive IL problem in which the objective is to match the expert's return distribution in Wasserstein distance. We focus on the tabular setting and assume the expert's reward is known. After demonstrating the limited expressivity of Markovian policies for this task, we introduce an efficient and sufficiently expressive subclass of non-Markovian policies tailored to it. Building on this subclass, we develop two provably efficient algorithms, RS-BC and RS-KT, for solving the problem when the transition model is unknown and known, respectively. We show that RS-KT achieves substantially lower sample complexity than RS-BC by exploiting dynamics information. We further demonstrate the sample efficiency of return distribution matching in the setting where the expert's reward is unknown by designing an oracle-based variant of RS-KT. Finally, we complement our theoretical analysis of RS-KT and RS-BC with numerical simulations, highlighting both their sample efficiency and the advantages of non-Markovian policies over standard sample-efficient IL algorithms."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12026","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:08Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:17:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12022","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12022","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12022","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eliott","familyName":"Pradeleix","name":"Pradeleix, Eliott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rémy","familyName":"Hosseinkhan-Boucher","name":"Hosseinkhan-Boucher, Rémy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alena","familyName":"Shilova","name":"Shilova, Alena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Onofrio","familyName":"Semeraro","name":"Semeraro, Onofrio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lionel","familyName":"Mathelin","name":"Mathelin, Lionel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning non-Markovian Dynamical Systems with Signature-based Encoders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:01:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:30:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural ordinary differential equations offer an effective framework for modeling dynamical systems by learning a continuous-time vector field. However, they rely on the Markovian assumption - that future states depend only on the current state - which is often untrue in real-world scenarios where the dynamics may depend on the history of past states. This limitation becomes especially evident in settings involving the continuous control of complex systems with delays and memory effects. To capture historical dependencies, existing approaches often rely on recurrent neural network (RNN)-based encoders, which are inherently discrete and struggle with continuous modeling. In addition, they may exhibit poor training behavior. In this work, we investigate the use of the signature transform as an encoder for learning non-Markovian dynamics in a continuous-time setting. The signature transform offers a continuous-time alternative with strong theoretical foundations and proven efficiency in summarizing multidimensional information in time. We integrate a signature-based encoding scheme into encoder-decoder dynamics models and demonstrate that it outperforms RNN-based alternatives in test performance on synthetic benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at [ML-DE] Machine Learning Meets Differential Equations 2025 (ECAI 2025). To appear in Proceedings of Machine Learning Research (PMLR)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12022","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:17:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:17:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:17:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12019","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12019","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12019","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangjun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sangjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seung-taek","familyName":"Woo","name":"Woo, Seung-taek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jungyu","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jungyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Changhun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunhyeok","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Eunhyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AMQ: Enabling AutoML for Mixed-precision Weight-Only Quantization of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:59:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:30:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To enable broader deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs), it is essential to identify the best-performing model under strict memory constraints. We present AMQ, Automated Mixed-Precision Weight-Only Quantization, a framework that assigns layer-wise quantization bit-widths to optimally balance model quality and memory usage. However, the combinatorial search space, with over 10^{100} possible configurations, makes conventional black-box optimization infeasible. AMQ overcomes this challenge through four key innovations:(1) search space pruning using prior knowledge to exclude unpromising configurations, (2) quantization proxy to bypass costly format conversions during search, (3) quality predictor to minimize evaluation overhead, and (4) iterative search-and-update strategy for fast and stable convergence. By integrating these components, AMQ efficiently explores the quality-efficiency landscape, reaching the Pareto frontier and yielding LLMs that are both compact and high-performing. Our code is available at https://github.com/dlwns147/amq."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Main Conference, Long Paper (Oral)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12019","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:16:57Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:16:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:16:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12010","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12010","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12010","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filippo","familyName":"Lazzati","name":"Lazzati, Filippo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto Maria","familyName":"Metelli","name":"Metelli, Alberto Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalizing Behavior via Inverse Reinforcement Learning with Closed-Form Reward Centroids"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:53:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:29:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the problem of generalizing an expert agent's behavior, provided through demonstrations, to new environments and/or additional constraints. Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL) offers a promising solution by seeking to recover the expert's underlying reward function, which, if used for planning in the new settings, would reproduce the desired behavior. However, IRL is inherently ill-posed: multiple reward functions, forming the so-called feasible set, can explain the same observed behavior. Since these rewards may induce different policies in the new setting, in the absence of additional information, a decision criterion is needed to select which policy to deploy. In this paper, we propose a novel, principled criterion that selects the \"average\" policy among those induced by the rewards in a certain bounded subset of the feasible set. Remarkably, we show that this policy can be obtained by planning with the reward centroid of that subset, for which we derive a closed-form expression. We then present a provably efficient algorithm for estimating this centroid using an offline dataset of expert demonstrations only. Finally, we conduct numerical simulations that illustrate the relationship between the expert's behavior and the behavior produced by our method."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12010","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:16:44Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:16:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:16:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11986","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11986","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11986","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengzu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Caiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Vulić","name":"Vulić, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anders","familyName":"Søgaard","name":"Søgaard, Anders","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lost in Embeddings: Information Loss in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:38:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:28:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision--language models (VLMs) often process visual inputs through a pretrained vision encoder, followed by a projection into the language model's embedding space via a connector component. While crucial for modality fusion, the potential information loss induced by this projection step and its direct impact on model capabilities remain understudied. We introduce two complementary approaches to examine and quantify this loss by analyzing the latent representation space. First, we evaluate semantic information preservation by analyzing changes in k-nearest neighbor relationships between image representations, before and after projection. Second, we directly measure information loss by reconstructing visual embeddings from the projected representation, localizing loss at an image patch level. Experiments reveal that connectors substantially distort the local geometry of visual representations, with k-nearest neighbors diverging by 40--60\\% post-projection, correlating with degradation in retrieval performance. The patch-level embedding reconstruction provides interpretable insights for model behavior on visually grounded question-answering tasks, finding that areas of high information loss reliably predict instances where models struggle."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11986","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:16:07Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:16:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:16:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11973","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11973","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11973","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus J.","familyName":"Buehler","name":"Buehler, Markus J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MusicSwarm: Biologically Inspired Intelligence for Music Composition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:23:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:27:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We show that coherent, long-form musical composition can emerge from a decentralized swarm of identical, frozen foundation models that coordinate via stigmergic, peer-to-peer signals, without any weight updates. We compare a centralized multi-agent system with a global critic to a fully decentralized swarm in which bar-wise agents sense and deposit harmonic, rhythmic, and structural cues, adapt short-term memory, and reach consensus. Across symbolic, audio, and graph-theoretic analyses, the swarm yields superior quality while delivering greater diversity and structural variety and leads across creativity metrics. The dynamics contract toward a stable configuration of complementary roles, and self-similarity networks reveal a small-world architecture with efficient long-range connectivity and specialized bridging motifs, clarifying how local novelties consolidate into global musical form. By shifting specialization from parameter updates to interaction rules, shared memory, and dynamic consensus, MusicSwarm provides a compute- and data-efficient route to long-horizon creative structure that is immediately transferable beyond music to collaborative writing, design, and scientific discovery."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11973","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:48Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxiao","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Mingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hewei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinfeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jinfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huilin","familyName":"Tai","name":"Tai, Huilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spec-LLaVA: Accelerating Vision-Language Models with Dynamic Tree-Based Speculative Decoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:16:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) enable powerful multimodal reasoning but suffer from slow autoregressive inference, limiting their deployment in real-time applications. We introduce Spec-LLaVA, a system that applies speculative decoding to accelerate VLMs without sacrificing output quality. Spec-LLaVA pairs a lightweight draft VLM with a large target model: the draft speculates future tokens, which the target verifies in parallel, allowing multiple tokens to be generated per step. To maximize efficiency, we design a dynamic tree-based verification algorithm that adaptively expands and prunes speculative branches using draft model confidence. On MS COCO out-of-domain images, Spec-LLaVA achieves up to 3.28$\\times$ faster decoding on LLaVA-1.5 (7B, 13B) with no loss in generation quality. This work presents a lossless acceleration framework for VLMs using dynamic tree-structured speculative decoding, opening a path toward practical real-time multimodal assistants. Importantly, the lightweight draft model design makes the framework amenable to resource-constrained or on-device deployment settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7pages, accepted by ICML TTODLer-FM workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11959","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11959","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11959","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youquan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Youquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyue","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Dongyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingdong","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Lingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaici","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Huaici","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei Tsang","familyName":"Ooi","name":"Ooi, Wei Tsang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Generate 4D LiDAR Sequences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:14:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:26:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While generative world models have advanced video and occupancy-based data synthesis, LiDAR generation remains underexplored despite its importance for accurate 3D perception. Extending generation to 4D LiDAR data introduces challenges in controllability, temporal stability, and evaluation. We present LiDARCrafter, a unified framework that converts free-form language into editable LiDAR sequences. Instructions are parsed into ego-centric scene graphs, which a tri-branch diffusion model transforms into object layouts, trajectories, and shapes. A range-image diffusion model generates the initial scan, and an autoregressive module extends it into a temporally coherent sequence. The explicit layout design further supports object-level editing, such as insertion or relocation. To enable fair assessment, we provide EvalSuite, a benchmark spanning scene-, object-, and sequence-level metrics. On nuScenes, LiDARCrafter achieves state-of-the-art fidelity, controllability, and temporal consistency, offering a foundation for LiDAR-based simulation and data augmentation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Abstract Paper (Non-Archival) @ ICCV 2025 Wild3D Workshop; GitHub Repo at https://lidarcrafter.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11959","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:28Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11942","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11942","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11942","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luís F.","familyName":"Gomes","name":"Gomes, Luís F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Abreu","name":"Abreu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VisDocSketcher: Towards Scalable Visual Documentation with Agentic Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T14:02:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:25:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual documentation is an effective tool for reducing the cognitive barrier developers face when understanding unfamiliar code, enabling more intuitive comprehension. Compared to textual documentation, it provides a higher-level understanding of the system structure and data flow. Developers usually prefer visual representations over lengthy textual descriptions for large software systems. Visual documentation is both difficult to produce and challenging to evaluate. Manually creating it is time-consuming, and currently, no existing approach can automatically generate high-level visual documentation directly from code. Its evaluation is often subjective, making it difficult to standardize and automate. To address these challenges, this paper presents the first exploration of using agentic LLM systems to automatically generate visual documentation. We introduce VisDocSketcher, the first agent-based approach that combines static analysis with LLM agents to identify key elements in the code and produce corresponding visual representations. We propose a novel evaluation framework, AutoSketchEval, for assessing the quality of generated visual documentation using code-level metrics. The experimental results show that our approach can valid visual documentation for 74.4% of the samples. It shows an improvement of 26.7-39.8% over a simple template-based baseline. Our evaluation framework can reliably distinguish high-quality (code-aligned) visual documentation from low-quality (non-aligned) ones, achieving an AUC exceeding 0.87. Our work lays the foundation for future research on automated visual documentation by introducing practical tools that not only generate valid visual representations but also reliably assess their quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11942","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:15:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:15:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:15:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":37,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=doi%3A%2810.48550%2Farxiv.2509.11942+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.11959+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.11961+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.11963+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.11967+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.11973+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.11983+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.11986+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12010+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12019+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12022+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12024+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12026+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12040+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12052+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12060+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12065+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12087+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12108+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12112+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12129+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12132+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12145+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12171+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12190+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12201+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.12203+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.13357+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.14260+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.14268+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.14269+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18133+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.18137+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.19325+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.19332+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.19336+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.22674%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1"}}